{"id":"u-ca2d0724-71e6-4d99-8f42-f64b74fd844f","src":"community","title":"Wendler 5/3/1 Boring But Big: Beefcake","author":"Brent Williams","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/HoWhV3-wendler-531-boring-but-big-beefcake","desc":"Boring But Big is the top monkey for two reasons. First, it is simple to follow and truly lives up to its name: boring. Second, it does a good job of putting on strength and size. Getting bigger, even if you don’t follow Boring But Big requires a few adjustments that are time-tested.Your main work must be proportional to your supplemental work. In the case of Boring But Big, the supplemental work is hard (as in a lot of volume is done with a “big” movement.) Because of this, the main work (main sets of the program) must be done in a limited manner. In general, all we are doing is trying to maintain the heavy weights and using the volume of Boring But Big (BBB) to raise strength.\n\nYour conditioning must be proportional to your lifting. The more volume and work you do in the weight room must be reflected by doing LESS strenuous conditioning work. In the case of getting bigger, you are not using conditioning for any other reason other than improved aerobic ability and recovery. Too many people try do too much of both and shit the bed on both. So your conditioning has to be such that it doesn’t break your body down so much that you can’t recover from the work in the weight room.\n\nYour calories have to reflect your volume and your goals. Getting bigger is no different than getting stronger or becoming a better athlete in terms of principles. You eat for performance and if your “performance” is getting bigger (more muscle mass) then you have to eat enough food to illicit recovery and to give your body fuel. It’s that simple (in principle).\n\nYour assistance work (this is the work that is done AFTER the main lift and the supplemental) has to be proportional also. Again, because we are doing a “hard” amount of supplemental work, you have to leave yourself some room to recover for the next workout. One thing that we’ve been experimenting with is using something like this for the BBB. Note: Main lifts are still done 5/3/1 style to preserve the philosophy of smart and progressive programming","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Athletics","Strength","Muscle"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":50,"joins":47,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-11-01T09:45:10.923Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3","Day 4"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":65},{"r":5,"pct":75},{"r":5,"pct":85}]},{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":50,"x":5}]},{"n":"Dip (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]},{"n":"Chin-Up (Assisted)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Overhead Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":65},{"r":5,"pct":75},{"r":5,"pct":85}]},{"n":"Overhead Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":50,"x":5}]},{"n":"Ab Wheel","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]},{"n":"Single-Leg Leg Curl","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":65},{"r":5,"pct":75},{"r":5,"pct":85}]},{"n":"Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":50,"x":5}]},{"n":"Dip (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]},{"n":"Face Pull","sets":[{"r":15,"x":7}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":65},{"r":5,"pct":75},{"r":5,"pct":85}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":50,"x":5}]},{"n":"Lying Leg Raise","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]},{"n":"Single Leg Lying Leg Curl","sets":[{"r":12,"x":3}]}]}],[{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":3,"pct":70},{"r":3,"pct":80},{"r":3,"pct":90}]},{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":50,"x":5}]},{"n":"Dip (Weighted)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]},{"n":"Chin-Up (Assisted)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Overhead Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":3,"pct":70},{"r":3,"pct":80},{"r":3,"pct":90}]},{"n":"Overhead Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":50,"x":5}]},{"n":"Ab Wheel","sets":[{"r":10,"x":5}]},{"n":"Sin